    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "Rider and driver: 1.56 SGD of this trip's charge is returned to the rider. The rest of the fare stands; the 5.00 SGD asked for is not the measure this policy uses.\n\nWhat the record shows. The app's recommended route was 12.1 km (Punggol Central, TPE, KPE, Paya Lebar Road); the route actually driven was 13.8 km, because the driver left the recommended route at the TPE / Upper Serangoon Road exit and ran down Upper Serangoon Road before rejoining at Paya Lebar Road. The deviation is 1.70 km (13.8 - 12.1), above POL-RD-22's 1.0 km tolerance. Both distances are the platform's own recorded figures on a complete trip record (EV-TRIP-05005, weight 1.0), so the deviation is established and the tolerance rows of POL-RD-24 (rows (c) and (e)) are closed.\n\nThe informing duty. POL-RD-21 requires the driver to tell the rider of a reroute by in-app chat before or at the point of deviation. The event log in EV-TRIP-05005 times the departure from the recommended route at 10:21:30, and the driver-device trace (EV-GPS-05005, weight 1.0) places the vehicle off-route from the 10:21:38 fix. The driver's message MSG-05005-003 was sent at 10:24:30 - about three minutes after the deviation had already begun - so the notice was not given before or at the point of deviation. The rider's 'ok noted' at 10:25:00 shows he did not object, but it cannot make a late notice timely: row (a) of POL-RD-24 requires the rider to have been informed as well as agreed, and the informing limb was not met. The driver's account that he informed the passenger 'before I changed route' is contradicted by those timestamps and by the empty in-app call list, so I do not follow it. The driver's argument that notice given while the deviation was still running is notice 'at the point of deviation' is rejected: the clause fixes the point of deviation as the moment of leaving the route.\n\nTraffic justification. The LTA record (EV-ADVIS-05005-01, weight 1.0) shows a VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN on the KPE, in the direction of travel, valid 09:59:38 to 10:54:38 on the trip date - a type POL-RD-23 admits as an 'incident' and a window that spans the trip (10:14:38-10:38:08) and lies on the KPE stretch the recommended route uses. I read that as traffic justification, which puts the case on row (b) of POL-RD-24. Even if the advisory were read as sitting past the point at which the recommended route leaves the KPE, the case would fall on row (d): both rows return the excess distance fare, so the outcome does not turn on it, nothing needs escalating under POL-GEN-26, and the rider's point that the refund is the same either way is accepted.\n\nThe amount. POL-RD-24 measures the refund as the deviation distance times the per-km rate, without surge: 1.70 km x 0.92 SGD/km = 1.564, rounded to the cent, 1.56 SGD (EV-FARE-05005; per_km_rate_sgd 0.92, distance_charged_km 13.8, total_charged 23.04, refund_issued_sgd 0.0, and the ledger's own derived excess_distance_fare_sgd of 1.56). The 5.00 SGD claimed is the amount asked back, not the fare attributable to the deviation, and the whole trip fare is not returnable because the rider was carried to the destination and most of the route was as recommended; a full refund is not a value this category licenses. The driver's point that the claim cannot exceed 1.56 SGD is therefore accepted as to quantum.\n\nConfidence and who decides. The contested value is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band; neither profile carries a fraud flag (EV-RPROF-05005, EV-DPROF-05005); and POL-RD-25's 2 km limb is switched off, so an AI ruling is competent at the 0.80 confidence this category requires. Every artifact carrying the deciding facts is at full weight (1.0), and I hold the ruling at 0.85. No clause produces a referral in this case, and no amount is due to the driver."
